       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. Ibuprofen 1600 mg four times daily comes to 6,400 mg/day. That is twice the labeled maximum of 3,200 mg/day for adults under prescription. A single 1,600 mg dose is also double the usual maximum single dose of 800 mg. At this level the risks of GI bleeding, kidney injury, and cardiovascular events go up a lot, and normal kidney and liver function doesn't change that.\n\nHere are some options within the labeled limits:\n- **800 mg PO three times daily** (2,400 mg/day). This is a common regimen for inflammatory pain.\n- **800 mg PO four times daily** (3,200 mg/day). This is the labeled maximum.\n- **400\u2013600 mg PO every 6 hours** (1,600\u20132,400 mg/day). This is typical for mild to moderate pain.\n\nIf the 1600 mg figure was a typo, for example 1600 mg/day total or 400 mg four times daily, tell me the intended dose and I'll enter it. If you need more analgesia than the maximum gives, I can enter a scheduled acetaminophen order alongside it. Acetaminophen is a separate drug, so it doesn't add to the ibuprofen total. Your team could also look at other options."
      }
     ],
